<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tanit</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tanit was the chief goddess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> and the Punic world, often paired with the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baal-hammon">Baal Hammon</a> and called 'face of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baal">Baal</a>'. Her emblem, a triangle topped by a horizontal bar and a disc, appears on thousands of stelae, coins and mosaics from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> and Ibiza. She was associated with fertility and protection, and under Roman rule her cult continued as that of Caelestis.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tanit.jpg" alt="Tanit" width="320" height="251" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ArchaiOptix · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carthago_-_350-250_BC_-_silver_tetradrachm_-_head_of_Tanit_-_head_of_horse_and_date_palm_-_M%C3%BCnchen_SMS.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Tanit, or Tinnit, was a chief deity of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> and consort of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baal-hammon">Baal Hammon</a>. Before 1955 her name was attested only in Punic, written without vowels as TNT or TNNT. Punic inscriptions in Greek letters found that year at El-Hofra, near Constantine in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, give it as Thinith and Thenneith, which indicates that it was pronounced Tinnīt, though many scholars still write Tanit. Hvidberg-Hansen argued that her origins lie in the pantheon of Ugarit, especially the goddess Anat.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> she did not appear before the 5th century BCE, and from then her worship was associated with that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baal-hammon">Baal Hammon</a>. She soon eclipsed him, and in the Carthaginian area she was often named before him on monuments. Inscriptions give her the epithet pene baal, &quot;face of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baal">Baal</a>&quot;, and the title rabat, &quot;chief&quot; or &quot;lady&quot;.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, where inscriptions and remains are plentiful, she was a heavenly goddess of war, a virginal mother goddess and nurse, and a symbol of fertility. She was also seen as defender of towns and homes. Her sign is a triangle, standing for the body, topped by a circle for the head and separated from it by a horizontal bar for the arms. Hvidberg-Hansen read it as a woman raising her hands.</p><p>She is also shown riding a lion or with a lion's head, winged, or riding a bull, and the lion, dove, palm and rose are linked with her. Worship spread across the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a> and Gades, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthaginians</a> carried it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a> with the founding of Gadir. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> identified her with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a>, and she was also identified with the Egyptian Neith.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>After the Roman conquest Tanit was merged with Juno, with elements of Diana and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minerva">Minerva</a>, as Dea Caelestis, just as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baal-hammon">Baal Hammon</a> was assimilated to Saturn. Dea Caelestis kept Punic traits until the end of classical antiquity in the 4th century CE, and her temple was among the biggest monuments of Roman Carthage, a holy site for pilgrims from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tophet">Tophet</a> of Salammbó at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>, found in 1921, held about 20,000 urns of burned bones of newborns and animals deposited between 400 and 200 BCE. Some archaeologists, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lawrence-stager">Lawrence Stager</a>, hold that these are the remains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/child-sacrifice">child sacrifice</a> to Tanit and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baal-hammon">Baal Hammon</a>. Others, such as Sergio Ribichini, argue that it was a necropolis for infants who died naturally. Patricia Smith and colleagues argue that the ages at death do not match natural mortality. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustave-flaubert">Gustave Flaubert</a>'s novel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salammbo">Salammbô</a> of 1862 has a priestess of Tanit as its title character.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tanit” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Religion blended Berber cults with Punic gods such as Baal Hammon and <b>Tanit</b>, and the kings themselves received cult after death.</blockquote><a href="/empire/numidia">Numidia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In the later account of Philo of Byblos, said to come from Sanchuniathon of Berytus, the creator Elion was father of the gods and married Beruth, “the city”, as other gods were linked with theirs: Melqart with Tyre, Chemosh with Moab, <b>Tanit</b> and Baal Hammon with Carthage.</blockquote><a href="/religion/canaanite-religion">Canaanite religion · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li><li><blockquote>Iberian religion was polytheistic and shaped by Greek, Carthaginian and Phoenician practice, and <b>Tanit</b>, Baal, Melqart, Artemis, Demeter and Asclepius were known and worshipped in the region.</blockquote><a href="/culture/iberians">Iberians · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>The Puig des Molins necropolis contains thousands of tombs, and the cave sanctuary of Es Culleram was dedicated to <b>Tanit</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/ebusus">Ebusus</a></li><li><blockquote>Its gods were the Phoenician Baal and <b>Tanit</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/utica">Utica</a></li></ul></section>
